DEQUE (DOUBLE-ENDED QUEUE) MASTERCLASS

====================================================================
1. FOUNDATIONAL EXPLANATION
====================================================================

1.1 What Is a Deque?
- A deque (pronounced “deck”) is a linear data structure that allows insertion and deletion at both the front and the back in O(1) amortized or worst-case time depending on implementation.
- Internally it can be implemented as a circular array, a block-linked list, or a doubly linked list. The key design goal is to keep head and tail operations symmetric.
- Within the hierarchy of linear structures, a deque generalizes both a queue (FIFO) and a stack (LIFO). By constraining operations to one end you recover those specialized structures.

1.2 Core Operations and Behavior
- push_front(x): insert element x at the front.
- push_back(x): insert element x at the rear.
- pop_front(): remove and return the front element.
- pop_back(): remove and return the rear element.
- peek_front()/peek_back(): read front/rear without removing.
- size(): number of elements currently stored.
- empty(): boolean check for zero elements.
- These operations guarantee constant-time behavior if the underlying representation keeps direct references to both ends (either via head/tail indices or pointers).

1.3 Time Complexity Summary
- push_front / push_back: O(1).
- pop_front / pop_back: O(1).
- peek_front / peek_back: O(1).
- size / empty: O(1).
- Iterating through all elements still costs O(n) like any linear structure.
- Why deques can beat stacks/queues in practice: you avoid data copying when you need both-direction expulsion, you can maintain sliding windows efficiently, and you reduce memory churn when toggling between front/back operations.

1.4 When to Reach for a Deque Instead of Stack/Queue
- Sliding window problems where you repeatedly add to one side and remove from the other.
- Maintaining maximum/minimum or monotonic order for online data streams.
- Simulating both ends of sequences (palindrome checks, undo/redo buffers).
- BFS variations where you push high-priority states to the front and low-priority states to the back (0–1 BFS).

====================================================================
2. IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS
====================================================================

2.1 Language Support Overview
- Java: `ArrayDeque<E>` from `java.util` offers resizable-array-backed deque with amortized O(1) operations. It forbids null values to avoid ambiguity between empty slots and actual data.
- Python: `collections.deque` uses a doubly-linked list of fixed-size blocks (a segmented linked list) yielding O(1) appends/pops on either side and thread-safe atomic appends/pops.
- C++: `std::deque<T>` is typically implemented as a map of contiguous blocks (random access allowed). It supports O(1) push/pop on both ends, and stable iterators except when insertion happens in the middle.

2.2 Array-Based vs Linked-Based Deques
- Array-Based (e.g., Java ArrayDeque):
	• Pros: excellent cache locality, predictable performance, smaller memory overhead, fast random access for debugging.
	• Cons: reallocation or block copying when growth exceeds current capacity; cannot easily splice or insert in the middle.
- Linked-Based (e.g., classic doubly linked list):
	• Pros: no resizing cost, constant-time concatenation if you maintain pointers, better for very large elements where pointer manipulation is cheaper than copying.
	• Cons: poor cache coherence, higher per-element memory footprint, pointer chasing can slow down real performance.
- Hybrid Block Deques (e.g., Python, C++): aim to capture the best of both worlds: low amortized reallocation cost while still providing decent locality.

